    "last_assistant_text": "Wait, in the vulnerable version (00ac71f9), after `syntax_error(\"identifier too long\")`, it falls through to `yr_strdup` and `return _IDENTIFIER_`. So even with a too-long identifier, the `c_string` is properly allocated. The `syntax_error` just sets an error and calls `yyerror`, but then proceeds normally.\n\nThe difference in the fix is that the fix adds `yyterminate()`, so the lexer terminates immediately on any error. Without it, after any error, `compiler->last_result` is set to an error code, and the next `fail_if` in the grammar fires.\n\nLet me look at the fix commit message again: \"the parser continues executing while some tokens can have associated invalid data (for example an _IDENTIFIER_ token with a NULL c_string)\"\n\nThis suggests the NULL c_string case. In the vulnerable version at lines 404-405, if `yr_strdup` returns NULL, `error(ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY)` is called but the lexer still returns `_IDENTIFIER_` with a NULL `c_string`. The parser then tries to use this NULL pointer.\n\nBut wait, that's an out-of-memory condition, which is hard to trigger from fuzzing input. The double-free might be triggered differently.\n\nLet me look at the `_STRING_IDENTIFIER_` case too:\n\nLines 311-318: If `yr_strdup` returns NULL, same issue. But again, OOM is hard to trigger.\n\nLet me look at the actual integer overflow path more carefully. When the lexer encounters an integer overflow (e.g., `99999999999999999999`):\n\n1. `error(ERROR_INTEGER_OVERFLOW)` at line 421\n2. `compiler->last_result = ERROR_INTEGER_OVERFLOW`\n3. `yyerror(yyscanner, compiler, NULL)`\n4. Falls through to `return _NUMBER_`\n5. Parser receives `_NUMBER_` token\n\nNow, `_NUMBER_` has type `<integer>` in the grammar, and there's no `%destructor` for it (it's not a pointer). So the `_NUMBER_` token itself isn't a problem. But the `compiler->last_result` is set to an error, which will trigger the next `fail_if`.\n\nThe question is: what's on the parser stack when `fail_if` fires? And does the error recovery path cause a double-free?\n\nLet me trace through a specific scenario:\n\n```\nrule a { condition: for any i in (99999999999999999999..1) : (true) }\n```\n\n1. `rule` → `_RULE_`\n2. `a` → `_IDENTIFIER_` (allocated c_string)\n3. `{` → shift\n4. `condition` → `_CONDITION_`\n5. `:` → shift\n6. `for` → `_FOR_`\n7. `any` → reduces to `for_expression`\n8. `i` → `_IDENTIFIER_` (allocated c_string \"i\")\n9. `in` → `_IN_`\n10. `(` → shift\n11. `99999999999999999999` → lexer: `error(ERROR_INTEGER_OVERFLOW)`, sets `compiler->last_result`, then returns `_NUMBER_`\n12. Parser reduces `_NUMBER_` to `primary_expression` using line 1598-1607\n13. At line 1598-1607, `fail_if(compiler->last_result != ERROR_SUCCESS)` fires at line 1603\n\nWait, let me check: does the `primary_expression` rule for `_NUMBER_` have a `fail_if`?",
